8398 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 23361 0 R
/E (Polymorphism in Object-Oriented Programming \(OOP\) is the ability of objects to take on multiple forms or behaviors, allowing different objects to be treated as instances of a common parent class while retaining their unique implementations. This core OOP concept enables code to be more flexible, reusable, and maintainable by providing a single interface to diverse underlying classes, making programs adaptable to new situations and easier to manage.  )
/Pg 123 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
